<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Khaldun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1332">1332</a>–<a href="/years/1406">1406</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Abd al-Rahman ibn Khaldun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Khaldun was an Arab historian and thinker born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1332 to a family of Andalusi origin. He served rulers in Tunis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> before writing his universal history, whose introduction, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqaddimah">Muqaddimah</a>, explains the rise and fall of dynasties through asabiyya, or group solidarity. He spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> judge and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1401.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun.jpg" alt="Ibn Khaldun" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reda Kerbush · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Ibn_Khaldun_(Casbah_of_Bejaia,_Algeria).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
